Dear Greg,

Thank you very much for the reply.

1) I find xml in general useful, even if it has some quirks/shortcomings. Unfortunately, there are various limitations arising due to the xml-infrastructure available in R (packages XML and xml2). Furthermore, I am a little bit rusty in xml as well (and even more so with xslt).

Although the use case covered a non-statistical topic, there is room for improvement for the aforementioned packages.

I would encourage developers to enhance the packages with functionality in which the user specifies explicitly which xml nodes to extract. At least, I could not find such functionality.

Example:
nodes.extract = c("Node1", "Node3", "@AttrID", "Node1 at AttrTime"), where "node1", ... are the node names in the xml document and "@AttrID" is an attribute named "AttrID" of the root node, while "Node1 at AttrTime" is an attribute named "AttrTime" for the "Node1" node.

Using the XML package syntax:
x = xmlToDataFrame(x, extract.nodes = nodes.extract);

It is a lot faster to write than an explicit extraction, the code is more readable and should also run faster.

Note: setting attributes on xml nodes (especially ids) seemed so clever back in the day. But it is tedious to write explicit extraction code, if automated extraction is missing.

2. xml PDB
For completion and reference, I add a few more bits of information; the topic itself is more appropriate for Bioconductor.

After peaking through some xml files, I conclude that the id attribute may not be necessary. There are differences to the legacy pdb format. I have skipped reading the documentation for the xml pdb format, after my initial experience with the documentation for the new text format.

In the legacy pdb format, the ids specified the non-standard connectivity. The standard connections between atoms were not specified (and had to be implemented by the software). It seems that the xml pdb does NOT use the ids, but an implicit identification using chain id, residue id and atom type. I will need to check the actual documentation (when I have some time).

Note: the design of the pdb xml is horrible as well. I do not think that the documentation will shine, either.

Sincerely,

Leonard

